PATCH 553: the first 16 KB of DGROUP was never triaged -- six more byte variables, including the
mission-state discriminator.

Ghidra names a DGROUP variable by the segment it guessed, so ONE variable space has two spellings:

    DAT_2000_XXXX  ->  g_mem+0x2XXXX  ->  DGROUP offset 0x4000+XXXX    (DGROUP 0x4000..)
    DAT_1000_XXXX  ->  g_mem+0x1XXXX  ->  DGROUP offset XXXX-0xc000    (DGROUP 0x0000..0x3fff)

`tools/width_triage.py` only knew the first spelling, so patch 552's sweep silently skipped the whole
first 16 KB of DGROUP.  Two of the port's own patch comments prove the mapping: `DAT_1000_d8e4` is the
ready-queue head the asm calls `[0x18e4]`, and `DAT_1000_fe00` is `[0x3e00]`.  With the scanner
corrected, six more macros meet patch 552's criteria -- declared `undefined2`, byte-width references
only, no reference the scanner cannot decode, and a LIVE successor byte.

    DAT_1000_d549   DGROUP:0x1549   14 byte refs, successor referenced 12x
    DAT_1000_d54a   DGROUP:0x154a   12 byte refs, successor referenced  4x
    DAT_1000_d54b   DGROUP:0x154b    4 byte refs, successor referenced  5x
    DAT_1000_d54f   DGROUP:0x154f    5 byte refs, successor referenced  6x
    DAT_1000_c44a   DGROUP:0x044a    1 byte ref,  successor referenced  3x
    DAT_1000_de7a   DGROUP:0x1e7a    2 byte refs, successor referenced  2x

DGROUP:0x1549 is the MISSION-STATE discriminator -- the byte the engine sets to 0x1c on entering the
cockpit, 0x1e, 0x22, 0x14, 0x16 elsewhere, and that the shim reads to know it is in a mission
(`g_mem[0x1c000 + 0x1549] == 0x1c`).  Every one of its fourteen references is byte-width:

    c394: 80 3e 49 15 1e    cmpb $0x1e,0x1549
    c509: 80 3e 49 15 1c    cmpb $0x1c,0x1549
    c510: 80 3e 49 15 20    cmpb $0x20,0x1549
    3aa2: a0 49 15          mov  0x1549,%al
    23b4: 8a 1e 49 15       mov  0x1549,%bl

and 0x154a directly after it is a live byte with twelve of its own (`8a 0e 4a 15`, `a0 4a 15`,
`a2 4a 15`, `c6 06 4a 15 ..`), so writing 0x1549 as a word was overwriting it on every store --
`DAT_1000_d549 = 0x1c` at the cockpit transition, `= 0x1e`, `= 0x22`.  0x1549/0x154a/0x154b/0x154f is
one run of mode bytes, the same shape as DGROUP:0x6da4..0x6db0 in patch 547.

Every use of all six was audited for anything that depends on the wide read; all are byte-semantic
(`== '\x1e'`, `= 0x1c`, `= 0`, `= 0xff`, `& 0xf`).  `DAT_1000_de7a & 0xfff0` is unchanged in meaning:
the asm is `a0 7a 1e ; 24 f0` (`mov 0x1e7a,%al ; and $0xf0,%al`) and an unsigned char promotes to int,
so the wider mask still selects the same bits.  These six are declaration-only changes.

`build/fist_decomp.c` is the pristine decompile that `assemble` consumes, not a compiled translation
unit, so there is no second definition of these macros to keep in step.

Two `undefined4` macros in the same newly-visible range (DGROUP:0x1f91 and 0x1f92) are NOT in this
patch: they are written by BOTH `movw` and `movb`, and they overlap each other, so they need their
containing functions disassembled rather than a triage verdict.  board:0023 carries them.

board:0023

